{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "50",
    "case_summary": "A 38-year-old male ophthalmologist presented at the Ophthalmic Accident and Emergency Department with a five-hour history of a watery red eye on the right side. He had been in contact with several patients diagnosed with conjunctivitis in the days prior while working in the same department. The patient was prescribed chloramphenicol eye drops. Conjunctival swabs were collected and sent for microbiological analysis. The following day, the conjunctivitis became bilateral and purulent. Several hours after the conjunctivitis affected both eyes, the patient developed a sore throat. The swab cultures identified Streptococcus pneumoniae as the infective agent. Three days later, the patient experienced tenderness and swelling in the proximal interphalangeal joint of the left index finger, which worsened over the next few days. The patient developed a fever but did not experience chills or rigors. He was prescribed oral amoxicillin 500 mg three times daily and flucloxacillin 500 mg four times daily.\n\nPlain X-ray showed a little soft tissue swelling over the proximal interphalangeal joint of the left index finger. The patient was negative for antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (ANCA), rheumatoid factor (RF), anti-DNA antibodies and antinuclear antibodies; complement factors C3 and C4 were within the reference range. No pathogen could be grown in blood culture and joint aspiration yielded no synovial fluid. The patient was HLA-B27 negative.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
